  "id": "Compound::DB00786 - Gene::4314",
  "verbalization": "Marimastat, a small molecule inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple MMPs (Matrix Metalloproteinases) through distinct interactions with Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27. These MMPs are all part of the same functional group, participating in the degradation of extracellular matrix components. The shared participation in MMP activity by these three proteins suggests a common role in pathological processes such as tissue remodeling and inflammation. The consistent binding scores across these three distinct MMPs indicate a robust, mechanistically supported interaction between Marimastat and the MMP family. The presence of a shared downstream effect—Mmp3 being part of the MMP family—supports the idea that Marimastat may modulate MMP activity in a broader context, potentially influencing processes such as cancer progression or fibrosis. The integrated evidence suggests that Marimastat's interaction with MMPs is a well-defined and biologically relevant mechanism, aligning with the observed final scores and the shared functional pathway.",
